  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nToday - Tonight: Increasing Rain Chances North of I-90\n\nGOES-19 water vapor imagery depicts a fairly progressive \nshortwave trough moving through Manitoba which will be the next\nsystem to bring any precipitation potential into the region \n(20-60%). As this wave swings into the Upper Midwest later this \nafternoon and evening, much of the CAMs generally agree on \ninstigating an area of showers for areas near and north of I-90 \nalong a diffuse warm front and along the associated synoptic \ncold front with the aforementioned wave. However, given \nprecipitable water values slightly over 1\" and MUCAPE values of\naround 100 J/kg in the recent RAP, high confidence (80%+) for\nprecipitation amounts to remain limited. Evident in recent HREF\n(03.00Z) rainfall probabilities for over 0.25\" of only 10 to \n40%. Due to the progressive nature of this shortwave, the \nsynoptic cold front swings through the area fairly quickly with \nmuch of the precipitation potential exiting the local area \naround midnight.\n\nSunday - Monday: Frost Potential Sunday Night/Monday Morning\n\nAs we head into Sunday and Monday, northwesterly flow in the wake of \nthe aforementioned system will keep temperatures somewhat cool for \nSunday and Monday with highs in the 60s areawide. Supplementary \nshortwave upper-level ridging builds into the area as well with\naccompanying surface high pressure. Consequently, expecting \nskies to clear for much of the day on Sunday and Monday with \nwinds trending lighter. As a result, when considering decent NBM\nprobabilities (40-70%) for low temperatures below 37 degrees \nduring the morning hours on Monday and the aforementioned lack \nof sky cover, confidence for frost across portions of central \nand north-central Wisconsin is increasing during this timeframe \nand Frost Advisories may be needed in subsequent forecast \nupdates. Otherwise, the fairly profound area of surface high \npressure over the region during this period should mitigate any \nprecipitation potential. \n\nTuesday - Friday: Staying Dry, Near to Slightly Above Normal \nTemperatures\n\nThe synoptic pattern starting Tuesday will remain relatively \nsteady-state with some indications of increasing upper-level \nridging later into the week. Consequently, median high \ntemperatures in the NBM staying in the 60s to lower 70s. The\nrelatively persistent surface high pressure with a lack of any \nshortwave perturbations in northwesterly synoptic flow limits \nprecipitation potential through Friday. This is evident in low\n(<20%) grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) probabilities\nfor measurable precipitation through Friday.",
